# This file lets GitHub show a "Cite this repository" button and gives the
# project a stable, machine-readable citation. It is also what Zenodo reads when
# you archive a release to mint a DOI.
#
# TODO before you rely on this for a paper/DOI:
#   1. Confirm the author name below is exactly how you want to be cited
#      (use your real given/family name — this is inferred, not verified).
#   2. Add your ORCID if you have one (uncomment the `orcid:` line).
#   3. After you create a Zenodo DOI, put it in `doi:` and in
#      `preferred-citation` so the two agree.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "QuantumSafe — a scanner for quantum-vulnerable cryptography"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Danny
    family-names: Lichtenberger
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Danny-397/Quantum-Safe-Scan"
url: "https://quantumsafescan.com"
abstract: >-
  QuantumSafe scans source code across 11 languages for cryptography that
  quantum computers will break (RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman) or weaken (AES-128,
  SHA-256), scores the risk 0-100, and maps every finding to its NIST FIPS
  203/204/205 post-quantum replacement. It ships a hybrid AST + regex detection
  engine (100% precision / 100% recall on a labeled adversarial benchmark),
  cross-file taint analysis, dependency and lockfile scanning, and SARIF/CBOM
  exports, backed by a runnable Qiskit implementation of Shor's and Grover's
  algorithms and a from-scratch Learning-With-Errors (LWE) key-encapsulation
  mechanism.
keywords:
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - static analysis
  - cryptographic bill of materials
  - NIST FIPS 203
  - harvest-now-decrypt-later
  - lattice cryptography
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0
date-released: "2026-07-18"
